  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Monday)\nIssued at 131 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nWednesday into Thursday, the aforementioned upper level ridge will \nshift east as the large upper level trough/low across the western \nCONUS slowly advances northeast. Meanwhile, hurricane Simon is \nforecast to make landfall across the northwest coast of Mexico late \nWednesday. As the upper level trough tracks northeast, some mid and \nupper level moisture from Simon will get pulled northeast into West \nTexas. This will result in precipitable water values of 1.7 to 2 \ninches (or higher) across our far western counties and west into the \nPermian Basin and Trans-Pecos region. Given these factors, scattered \nto numerous showers and thunderstorms are possible late Wednesday \ninto Friday, mainly across our western and northern counties. Later \nThursday, as the aforementioned upper level low tracks northeast, \nthe main trough will remain to the west with an upper level low \ndeveloping across the Desert Southwest and slowly retrograding \nsouthwest. Meanwhile, a cold front will track south through the area \non Thursday, which will act as a lifting mechanism for showers and \nthunderstorms. The current forecast is for the heaviest rain to \nremain west of our area, but a slight shift east could place the \ncorridor of heaviest rain into our western counties. For now, have \ncontinued medium to high (40-90%) rain chicanes for this time frame. \nRain chances will be highest across our western counties and quickly \ndecrease as you head east. I wanted to add that there is the \npotential for most of the rainfall to remain west of our area. \nLatest guidance keeps our forecast area on the eastern edge of most \nof the rainfall. Will continue to monitor, but rain chances might \neventually need to be lowered if this trend continues. \n\nThe aforementioned cold front that is forecast to move through the \narea on Thursday will usher in breezy north winds, cooler \ntemperatures, and eventually drier conditions. Much cooler \ntemperatures are forecast Friday into the weekend, with highs in the \n60s and 70s. Overnight lows will mainly be in the 40s.",
